---
import GalleryLayout from "../../layouts/GalleryLayout.astro";
import NavbarSiteLayout from "../../layouts/NavbarSiteLayout.astro";
import GalleryAutographCard from "../../components/gallery-autograph-card.astro";
import { getCollection, type CollectionEntry } from 'astro:content';

const autographs: CollectionEntry<'autograph'>[] = await getCollection('autograph');
const tags = [
  ...new Set(
    autographs.flatMap((item) => item.data.categories)
  ),
].sort();
---

<NavbarSiteLayout title="Autograph Gallery" description="View Carole's autograph collection (available for purchase).">
  <GalleryLayout pageHeading="Autograph Gallery" tags={tags}>
    <Fragment slot="gallery-description">
      <p>I collected autographs starting in 1974 while I was a stay-at-home Mom with my twin girls. I bought a rather expensive list of movie star addresses and started the project. I wrote full-page letters to famous people telling them what I most admired about their work. I sent self-addressed stamped envelopes with an ad featuring the movie star, asking if they would autograph that image. Just because they were probably wealthy, they should not have to spend postage on my hobby, I thought.</p>

      <p>To Shelley Winters (whom I had just watched being interviewed on the Johnny Carson show) I personalized my comments by saying, “I think Johnny should have married you rather than the last bimbo he married.” His last wife had said she <em>absolutely must</em> have $40K a month for furs and jewels. Shelley struck me as a smart, blunt-talking woman who would have offered the very bright talk show host a more challenging interesting life, rather than that shallow woman.</p>

      <p>I guess I probably picked just the right time to do such a project, because these people were just coming down from the peaks of their careers. Their fan mail likely was going down by the 1970s. They had become yesterday’s news. I received a nearly 100 percent response. Sometimes they would personalize a message to me. I realize they could have had their secretary sign, but I do believe I had the real autograph of nearly all of them.</p>

      <p>Watching late-night movies, I realized what an extraordinary time the 1930s and ‘40s was for a certain type of script and acting that was gone by 1960. The new era of movies after the Golden Age of Hollywood was so hyper-realistic that I was made uncomfortable with dialogue that too much resembled and felt like real-life. The rapid back and forth of 1930s and ‘40s films carry the viewer along in a rush, pulling you along in a mesmerizing plot. That is, it did that in the good ones. I felt like I was stepping into another world.</p>

      <p>I had lived in a rough part of Los Angeles in the mid-1960s, and life there did feel bizarre there, especially being poor. I desperately wanted to be in college and not to be there! How I got stuck there was a bad story. Amazingly, I met several of the old stars.</p>

      <p>Betty Hutton loaned me furniture. I went to Beverly Hills Lutheran Church (and sang in the choir) where Robert Stack and Burt Lancaster attended. On a memorable Christmas Eve Pete Candoli the famous actor and composer played the trumpet for us. I met Robert Mitchum’s son on the beach when I was with a guy who had a small part in the Annette Funicello (really silly) movie, <em>Beach Blanket Bingo</em>.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="gallery-items">
      {
        autographs.map(item => (
          <GalleryAutographCard item={item} />
        ))
      }
    </Fragment>
  </GalleryLayout>

  <script>
    import "../../scripts/gallery-filter.js";
  </script>
</NavbarSiteLayout>